{"type": "sena", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": "All's Well That Ends Well"}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "What Are Friends For! "}, {"type": "position", "content": 4}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 16}, {"type": "level", "content": "B2.3"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe emotions and feelings in present and past situations in spoken or written texts and in work and social contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_2", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_3", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_4", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_5", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_6", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_7", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_8", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "engenn11le16ob04re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Emphatic Stress"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "When you want to call attention to something special that you want to emphasize in a sentence, you can change the stress from the main noun of the sentence, to another content word (adjective or adverb)."}, {"type": "text", "content": "To emphasize a word, you can change the stress from the principal word to another content word. By choosing a word other than the one you normally stress, you put emphasis on the new one.</br></br> For example: "}, {"type": "text", "content": "That was a difficult <span class=\"strong\">exam</span>. - <span class=\"italic\">Standard statement</span></br></br> That was a <span class=\"strong\">difficult</span> exam. - <span class=\"italic\">Emphasizes how difficult the exam was</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "The following adverbs or intensifiers are commonly used emphatically: extremely, terribly,completely, utterly,especially </br></br>Read these sentences aloud. Focus on the emphatic stress."}, {"type": "example", "content": " I was out with friends having some VEry boring conversation."}, {"type": "example", "content": "I don't know if I have anything TERRibly thought provoking to tell you."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Listening to them was UTTerly uncompelling. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Why was your discussion with your friends so eSPECIALly dull?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I was COMPLETely embarrassed."}, {"type": "example", "content": "It is EXTREMely short notice."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Contrastive Stress"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "There are various types of stress patterns used when speaking English."}, {"type": "text", "content": "When you want to point out the difference between one object and another, you use <span class=\"underline\">contrastive stress</span>. </br></br>  You tend to use contrastive stress with determiners such as <span class=\"italic\">this</span>, <span class=\"italic\">that</span>, <span class=\"italic\">these</span> and <span class=\"italic\">those</span>."}, {"type": "text", "content": " For example:   </br></br><span class=\"italic\">Do you like <span class=\"strong\">this</span> one or <span class=\"strong\">that</span> one? </br>I like <span class=\"strong\"> this</span> one.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "When you use contrastive stress, as with any other stress pattern, you need to raise the pitch and volume of your voice when you pronounce the stressed word.  </br></br>Practice saying these sentences from the dialogue:"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I was out with friends having some very boring conversation with them, so I thought I'd come here and have some interesting conversation with you instead."}, {"type": "example", "content": "My friends like to talk about all their possessions, whereas I prefer talking about experiences. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Is she married or single? "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Who does she like better shy guys or outgoing guys? "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Does she like tall guys or short guys?  "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Does she prefer Coke or Pepsi?"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "thought-provoking"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "stimulating careful consideration or attention"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I don't know if I have anything terribly thought provoking."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le16ob04aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use to try and convince someone to travel."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "uncompelling"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "not interesting"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Listening to them was utterly uncompelling."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le16ob04aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to traipse"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to walk wearily or with reluctance"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: This is not the best time to go traipsing around the world."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le16ob04aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "extended absence"}, {"type": "definition", "content": " a long period of time spent away from one's responsibilities"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You don't have to stay away for an extended absence."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le16ob04aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "getaway"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a vacation"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "  (noun) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Just for a nice little getaway. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le16ob04aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "frigid"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "very cold in temperature"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Sun and sand or a frigid Boston winter?"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le16ob04aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I create a roleplay with my partner."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I use Contrastive Stress questions to learn more information."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I use Emphatic Stress to answer my partner's questions."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "You have a friend who you'd like to set up with your partner."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Your partner is unsure and makes a list of ten contrastive questions to ask. ex) Is she tall or is she short? Does he like rock music or reggae music?"}, {"type": "text", "content": "You must persuade your partner emphasizing your friend's qualities.  ex)  She is extremely tall or he is very smart."}, {"type": "text", "content": "After the ten questions, your partner must decide if they were persuaded or not."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Switch roles."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}], "labs": [{"exercises": [{"questions": [], "img_path": "", "stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can talk about beliefs, opinions, relationships or character traits."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can emphasize different things in a statement using intonation."}, {"type": "Lea